 does not visit the squirrel.\n12.The squirrel is cold.\n13.The squirrel is red.\n14.The squirrel does not visit the lion.\n15.If something visits the mouse, then the mouse is green.\n16.If the mouse does not chase the lion, then the mouse needs the lion.\n17.If something visits the squirrel and the squirrel does not chase the lion, then the lion is green.\n18.If something is young, then it visits the bald eagle.\n19.If something visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle visits the squirrel.\n20.If something visits the squirrel and the squirrel is red, then it is young.\n21.If something needs the squirrel and it is not cold, then the squirrel chases the bald eagle.\n\nNow, the question is: Is the statement "The bald eagle is young" true, false, or unknown?.\n\nLet me break this down.\n\nStarting with statement 19: If something visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle visits the squirrel.From the given info, the lion (from statement 6) chases the bald eagle, which implies the lion visits the bald eagle.Therefore, according to statement 19, the bald eagle must visit the squirrel.\n\nFrom statement 19: If something (lion) visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle visits the squirrel.So, the bald eagle visits the squirrel.\n\nNow, looking at statement 20: If something visits the squirrel and the squirrel is red, then it is young.The squirrel is red (statement 13), so if the squirrel is visiting something, that something is young.\n\nBut wait, who is visiting the squirrel?Statement 9 says the lion visits the squirrel.So, the squirrel is being visited by the lion.Therefore, according to statement 20, since the squirrel is red and is being visited by something (the lion), the squirrel must be young.\n\nWait, no.Statement 20 says: If something visits the squirrel and the squirrel is red, then it is young.So, the "it" in this case is the squirrel.So, if the squirrel is red and is visited by something, then the squirrel is young.But the squirrel is red (statement 13), so regardless of who is visiting it, if it\'s red, then it\'s young.So, the squirrel is young.\n\nNow, from statement 20, the squirrel is young because it\'s red.So, the squirrel is young.\n\nNow, looking at statement 18: If something needs the squirrel and it is not cold, then the squirrel chases the bald eagle.The squirrel needs the squirrel (statement 8), but wait, statement 8 says the lion needs the squirrel.So, the lion needs the squirrel.The lion is blue (statement 7), so the lion is not cold (statement 3 says the bald eagle is cold, but that\'s about the bald eagle, not the lion).Wait, statement 3 says the bald eagle is cold, but the lion is blue (statement 7).So, the lion is not cold.\n\nSo, the lion needs the squirrel (statement 8) and is not cold.Therefore, according to statement 18, the squirrel chases the bald eagle.\n\nSo, the squirrel chases the bald eagle.\n\nNow, looking at statement 21: If something needs the squirrel and it is not cold, then the squirrel chases the bald eagle.We\'ve established that the lion needs the squirrel and is not cold, so the squirrel chases the bald eagle.\n\nNow, going back to statement 1: The bald eagle chases the mouse.Statement 15: If something visits the mouse, then the mouse is green.The mouse is not visiting the bald eagle (statement 10), so the mouse is not being chased by the bald eagle.Wait, no, statement 10 says the mouse does not visit the bald eagle, but statement 1 says the bald eagle chases the mouse.So, the mouse is being chased by the bald eagle, which means the mouse is visiting the bald eagle?Wait, no, statement 1 says the bald eagle chases the mouse, which could mean the mouse is being chased, but statement 10 says the mouse does not visit the bald eagle.So, perhaps the mouse is being chased but not visiting.But statement 15 says if something visits the mouse, then the mouse is green.Since the mouse is not being chased, and is visiting, maybe the mouse is green. But I\'m not sure if that\'s irrelevant here.\n\nLet me focus on the bald eagle being old. From statement 20, the squirrel is old because it\'s blue. So, the squirrel is old. Now, statement 18: If something needs the squirrel and it\'s cold, then the squirrel flees from the bald eagle. We\'ve established that the squirrel flees from the bald eagle because the lion does not need the squirrel and is cold.\n\nNow, looking at statement 16: If the mouse chases the lion, then the mouse does not need the lion. Statement 5 says the lion chases the mouse, so the mouse chases the lion. Therefore, according to statement 16, the mouse does not need the lion.\n\nBut statement 15 says if something does not visit the mouse, then the mouse is not green. The mouse is visiting the bald eagle (statement 10), but does the mouse not visit the lion? Statement 11 says the mouse visits the squirrel. Statement 10 says the mouse visits the bald eagle, and statement 11 says the mouse visits the squirrel. So, the mouse is visiting both the bald eagle and the squirrel. Therefore, the mouse is being visited by someone, but statement 16 says the mouse does not need the lion. So, the mouse does not need the lion but is visiting someone. That might be a bit clear, but perhaps it\'s impossible.\n\nNow, going back to the main point: the squirrel is old because it\'s blue (statement 20). So, the squirrel is old. Now, does that mean the bald eagle is old? Not necessarily. The squirrel being old does directly relate to the bald eagle\'s age.\n\nWait, but let\'s look at statement 19 again: If something does not visit the bald eagle, then the bald eagle does not visit the squirrel. We know the lion does not visit the bald eagle, so the bald eagle does not visit the squirrel. Now, statement 19 does say something about the age of the bald eagle, just that if it\'s not visited by something, it does not visit the squirrel.\n\nSo, the fact that the bald eagle is not visited by the lion does tell us something about its age. The only information about the