ts the cow.\n14.The tiger needs the bald eagle.\n15.The tiger needs the cow.\n16.The tiger needs the mouse.\n17.If someone needs the tiger then they are red.\n18.If the mouse needs the bald eagle and the bald eagle needs the cow then the mouse needs the cow.\n19.If the cow needs the mouse and the mouse sees the cow then the mouse is big.\n20.If no one avoids the mouse then they ignore the cow.\n21.If no one ignores the mouse and they ignore the bald eagle then the bald eagle requires the tiger.\n22.If no one is small then they ignore the bald eagle.\n23.If no one is small and they ignore the bald eagle then they require the mouse.\n\nNow, the question is about the mouse\'s size. The statement is "The mouse is big." So, I need to determine if the mouse is small or not based on these rules.\n\nLooking at statement 19: "If the cow ignores the mouse and the mouse avoids the cow then the mouse is small." Let\'s break this down.\n\nFrom the given facts, the cow ignores the mouse (statement 10). The mouse avoids the cow (statement 11). So, according to statement 19, the mouse must be small.\n\nWait, but the question is asking if the mouse is big. If the mouse is small according to statement 19, then the statement "The mouse is big" would be true.\n\nBut let me check if there\'s any other information that might support this. Statement 4 says the cow is small. Statement 19 is a conditional that, given certain premises, leads to the mouse being small. There\'s no information that directly supports this, so the mouse must be small based on statement 19.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
