o Anne is cold → Anne is furry.\n\nFrom point 10, if someone is furry, then they are green.So Anne is furry → Anne is green.\n\nFrom point 11, green people are black. So Anne is green → Anne is black.\n\nWait, but the question is about whether Anne is white. If Anne is black, then the statement "Anne is white" would be true. But let me double-check to make sure I didn\'t miss anything.\n\nLet me go through each step again:.\n\n- Anne is smart → Anne is old (from 9).\n- Anne is old → Anne is hot (from 8).\n- Anne is hot → Anne is smooth (from 15).\n- Anne is smooth → Anne is blue (from 10).\n- Anne is blue → Anne is black (from 11).\n\nSo yes, Anne is black. Therefore, the statement "Anne is white" would be true because Anne is black.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any other information that might affect this. For example, point 7 says Harry is blue, and point 6 says Harry is cold. But since all old people are hot (point 8), and Harry is old, that\'s fine. Also, point 13 says if Harry is blue and old, then Harry is smooth, but since Harry is old, that does apply. So Harry\'s color does affect Anne.\n\nAnother thing: point 16 says if Gary is blue, then Gary is smooth. But Gary is small and from point 5, he\'s smooth, so that\'s inconsistent because if Gary is blue, he\'s smooth, but we don\'t know if Gary is blue. However, point 14 says all hot people are smooth, which Anne is not, so that\'s inconsistent.\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
