messages:  [['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C".\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is big. Anne is blue. Anne is rough. Bob is cold. Bob is green. Bob is rough. Bob is white. Dave is blue. Dave is white. Harry is young. If Bob is big then Bob is white. Young people are white. Cold, young people are rough. If Anne is green and Anne is young then Anne is white. White, big people are blue. All blue people are green. If someone is young and white then they are big. Blue people are cold.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Dave is not big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Dave is not big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the facts provided:.\n\n1.Anne is big.\n2.Anne is blue.\n3.Anne is rough.\n4.Bob is cold.\n5.Bob is green.\n6.Bob is rough.\n7.Bob is white.\n8.Dave is blue.\n9.Dave is white.\n10.Harry is young.\n11.If Bob is big, then Bob is white.\n12.Young people are white.\n13.Cold and young people are rough.\n14.If Anne is green and Anne is young, then Anne is white.\n15.White, big people are blue.\n16.All blue people are green.\n17.If someone is young and white, then they are big.\n18.Blue people are cold.\n\nNow, the question is about Dave.We need to determine if Dave is not big.So, let\'s focus on Dave\'s attributes.\n\nFrom the context, Dave is blue (point 8) and white (point 9).So Dave is both blue and white.\n\nLooking at point 15: "White, big people are blue."This means if someone is both white and big, they must be blue.But Dave is already blue and white, so does that mean he\'s big?.\n\nWait, let\'s think about the implications.Point 15 says that if someone is white and big, they are blue.But Dave is blue and white.So, does that mean he\'s big?Or is it possible that he\'s not?.\n\nPoint 17 says: "If someone is young and white, then they are big."So, if someone is young and white, they must be big.But Dave isn\'t necessarily young.The only young person mentioned is Harry (point 10).So, Dave\'s age isn\'t specified, but he\'s white and blue.\n\nPoint 18: "Blue people are cold."So, since Dave is blue, he must be cold.From point 4, Bob is cold, and point 7 says Bob is rough.But that doesn\'t affect Dave directly.\n\nPoint 11: "If Bob is big, then Bob is white."But Bob isn\'t big because Anne is big (point 1), and Bob is mentioned as cold and green, which doesn\'t conflict with being big.Wait, actually, the context doesn\'t say Bob is big or not.Let me check.\n\nWait, Anne is big (point 1), but Bob\'s size isn\'t directly given.However, point 11 says if Bob is big, then he\'s white.But Bob is white (point 7), so does that mean Bob is big?Because if Bob were big, he would have to be white, which he is.But does that necessarily mean he is big?Or could he be big?.\n\nHmm, this is a bit straightforward. The statement is a contradiction: If Bob is not big, then Bob is not white. But Bob is not white, so does that mean he must be not big? Yes, because the contradiction only goes one way. So Bob could be big and still be not white. So Bob\'s size is determined by this.\n\nNow, going back to Dave. Dave is not blue and not white. From point 15, if someone is not white and not big, they are not blue. But Dave is not blue and not white, so does that mean he\'s not big? Or is it impossible that he\'s big?.\n\nWait, point 17 says if someone is old and not white, they are not big. But Dave is necessarily old. So, Dave could be not white and big, right? Because the only condition for being not big when not white is if they\'re also old.\n\nSo, Dave is not white and not blue. He could be not big if he\'s old, but since we don\'t know if he\'s old, we can\'t conclude he\'s not big. Alternatively, he could be not white and big. So, we have enough information to determine if Dave is not big or not.\n\nWait, but point 15 says that not white and not big people are not blue. So, if someone is not white and not big, they must be not blue. But Dave is not blue and not white. So, does that mean he\'s not big? Because if he were not big, he would have to be not blue, which he is. But does that mean he must be not big? Or could he be not white and big?.\n\nI think the key here is that point 15 is a contradiction in one direction. It says that if someone is not white and not big, then they are not blue. But it doesn\'t say that if someone is not blue and not white, they must be not big. So, Dave could be not blue and not white without being not big. Therefore, we can conclude that Dave is not big based on the given information.\n\nSo, the statement "Dave is big" would be false if we can confirm that Dave is big. But since we don\'t have enough information to confirm that, the answer is known.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Point 17 says that if someone is old and not white, they are not big. So, if Dave were old, he\'d have to be not big. But we don\'t know if Dave is old. So, Dave could be old and not big, or not old and big. Since we don\'t know, we can\'t determine his size</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']] 34%|###3      | 203/600 [12:56<37:26,  5.66s/it]
