 13 says if Harry is small, then he\'s warm. So Harry is neither small nor warm. Point 14 says small, foolish people are loud. But Harry\'s loudness is directly linked to his size and being foolish, except through this statement. However, point 5 says Gary is loud, and point 14 says small, foolish people are loud, but that necessarily affects Fiona.\n\nPoint 11 says if someone is loud and warm, then they are black. So if someone is both loud and warm, they\'re black. But Fiona is warm, and we don\'t know if she\'s loud. Point 2 says she\'s warm, but nothing about her being loud. So we can conclude she\'s black based on that.\n\nPoint 10 and 12 say all scaly people are black. So if someone is scaly, they\'re black. But we don\'t know if Fiona is scaly. Point 8 says if someone is black and foolish, they\'re scaly. So being black and foolish makes someone scaly, and we know Fiona is black and foolish.\n\nPoint 15 says if someone is small, they\'re foolish. So small people are foolish. But Fiona is mentioned as small. Point 4 says Gary is small, and point 6 says he\'s loud. But again, that\'s about Gary, not Fiona.\n\nSo, circling back to Fiona: she\'s warm (point 2) and smooth (point 3). The question is whether she\'s rough. Since point 3 says she is smooth, the statement "Fiona is rough" would be true.\n\nI do see many contradictions and missing information that would change this conclusion. All other points either affect Fiona\'s smoothness or are about other people.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
