messages:   50%|####9     | 299/600 [17:04<11:43,  2.34s/it][['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C".\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is furry. Anne is nice. Anne is rough. Anne is white. Erin is furry. Erin is rough. Erin is white. Fiona is big. Fiona is nice. Fiona is round. Harry is nice. Harry is rough. All furry things are white. If Harry is red then Harry is round. All round, red things are furry. If Harry is rough then Harry is round. Nice, red things are big. Round, red things are white. All nice, white things are red. If something is round then it is furry.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Harry is big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Harry is big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the facts provided:.\n\n1.Anne is furry, nice, rough, and white.\n2.Erin is furry, rough, and white.\n3.Fiona is big, nice, and round.\n4.Harry is nice and rough.\n5.All furry things are white.\n6.If Harry is red, then Harry is round.\n7.All round, red things are furry.\n8.If Harry is rough, then Harry is round.\n9.Nice, red things are big.\n10.Round, red things are white.\n11.All nice, white things are red.\n12.If something is round, then it is furry.\n\nNow, I need to determine if Harry is big.Let\'s break it down.\n\nFrom point 4, Harry is nice and rough.So, Harry is nice and rough.\n\nLooking at point 8: If Harry is rough, then Harry is round.Since Harry is rough, according to this statement, Harry must be round.\n\nNow, from point 10: Round, red things are white.So, if Harry is round and red, then it\'s white.But wait, from point 11: All nice, white things are red.So, if something is nice and white, it\'s red.\n\nBut let\'s see if Harry is red.From point 6: If Harry is red, then Harry is round.But we already know from point 8 that if Harry is rough, he\'s round.So, whether Harry is red or not, he\'s round.\n\nWait, but point 10 says that round, red things are white.So, if Harry is square and blue, then he\'s black. But from point 11, if Harry is mean and black, then he\'s blue. So, if Harry is mean and black, he\'s blue. But we do know yet if Harry is black.\n\nWait, from point 5: All smooth things are black. So, if Harry is smooth, he\'s black. But from point 4, Harry is mean and soft. We do know yet if Harry is smooth. Let\'s check point 12: If something is square, then it\'s smooth. Since Harry is square (from point 8), he must be smooth. Therefore, Harry is smooth, which means from point 5, Harry is black.\n\nNow, if Harry is black, then from point 11: All mean, black things are blue. So, since Harry is mean and black, he must be blue.\n\nSo, Harry is blue, black, mean, soft, and square.\n\nNow, from point 9: Mean, blue things are small. Since Harry is mean and blue, he must be small.\n\nTherefore, Harry is small.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Harry is mean and soft, so he\'s square (point 8). Being square means he\'s smooth (point 12). Being smooth means he\'s black (point 5). Being black and mean means he\'s blue (point 11). Being blue and mean means he\'s small (point 9). So no, Harry is small</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
