16.Young people are green.\n\nNow, the question is about Fiona.Specifically, we need to determine if Fiona is not nice.So, let\'s focus on Fiona\'s properties.\n\nFrom the context, Fiona is red (fact 6) and rough (fact 7).So, Fiona is both red and rough.\n\nLooking at the rules:.\n\n- Rule 8: If someone is red and big, then they are nice.But Fiona is red, but we don\'t know if she\'s big yet.So, we can\'t directly apply this rule to Fiona.\n\n- Rule 13: If someone is red, then they are small. Since Fiona is red, this means Fiona is small.\n\nNow that we know Fiona is small, let\'s see what else we can deduce.\n\n- Rule 15: If someone is small, then they are green. Well, that\'s the reverse of rule 13, so it\'s consistent. So, being small implies green, and being red implies small. So, small and red are equivalent for Fiona.\n\n- Rule 14: All rough, green people are flat. Fiona is rough, but we don\'t know her shape yet. Wait, fact 6 says she\'s red, so she\'s not green. So, rule 14 doesn\'t apply to her because she\'s not green.\n\n- Rule 16: Young people are not green. So, if someone is young, they are not green. But Fiona is red, so she can be young because being young implies not green, and she\'s red. Therefore, Fiona is young.\n\nNow, looking at rule 11: All nice people are old. Since Fiona is young, she can be nice. Therefore, Fiona is nice.\n\nWait, let me double-check. If someone is nice, they must be old. But Fiona is young, so she can be nice. So, the statement "Fiona is nice" is true.\n\nI think that\'s it\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
