int 10) and old (point 11). From point 12, not furry, not green things are red. So, Gary, being not furry and not green, should be red. But point 17 says if Gary is not furry, then Gary is red. That aligns with point 12, so that\'s consistent.\n\nAlso, point 19 says all mean things are old, and point 18 says all mean, small things are old. Since Gary is old, that fits. But I don\'t see any contradictions here.\n\nWait, but the question is asking if "Gary is furry" is true, false, or unknown. Since point 9 directly states that Gary is not furry, that should be false. But let me make sure I\'m not missing anything.\n\nIs there any rule that might say Gary is furry? Let me check. Point 17 says if Gary is not furry, then he\'s red. But there\'s no rule that says if Gary is furry, he can’t be something else. So, Gary could be not furry or not, but point 9 says he is not furry. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nWait, but maybe I\'m overcomplicating it. The question is straightforward: is Gary not furry? The context explicitly says Gary is not furry (point 9)\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
