messages:  [['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C".\n####\n# Context:\nThe cat is nice. The cat needs the lion. The cat visits the lion. The cow is nice. The cow needs the cat. The lion is nice. The lion is red. The lion is young. The lion needs the cow. The lion needs the squirrel. The squirrel is young. If the cow needs the lion then the lion visits the squirrel. If something needs the squirrel then it visits the cat. If something needs the cow and it chases the squirrel then the cow is rough. If something chases the squirrel and it is young then the squirrel needs the cat. If something visits the cow then it is rough. If the squirrel visits the cat and the cat is red then the squirrel chases the cow. All nice things are big. If something is big then it visits the squirrel. If something visits the cat and the cat visits the squirrel then the squirrel is nice.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The squirrel is not big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The squirrel is not big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given statements to make it easier to understand:.\n\n1.The cat is nice.\n2.The cat needs the lion.\n3.The cat visits the lion.\n4.The cow is nice.\n5.The cow needs the cat.\n6.The lion is nice.\n7.The lion is red.\n8.The lion is young.\n9.The lion needs the cow.\n10.The lion needs the squirrel.\n11.The squirrel is young.\n12.If the cow needs the lion, then the lion visits the squirrel.\n13.If something needs the squirrel, then it visits the cat.\n14.If something needs the cow and it chases the squirrel, then the cow is rough.\n15.If something chases the squirrel and it is young, then the squirrel needs the cat.\n16.If something visits the cow, then it is rough.\n17.If the squirrel visits the cat and the cat is red, then the squirrel chases the cow.\n18.All nice things are big.\n19.If something is big, then it visits the squirrel.\n20.If something visits the cat and the cat visits the squirrel, then the squirrel is nice.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the squirrel is not big.So, I need to determine if the squirrel is big or not based on these statements.\n\nLet me start by identifying what we know about the squirrel.From statement 11, the squirrel is young.Statement 10 says the lion needs the squirrel.Statement 12 says if the cow needs the lion, then the lion visits the squirrel.But from statement 9, the lion needs the cow, so according to statement 12, the lion would visit the squirrel.So, the lion visits the squirrel.\n\nFrom statement 13, if something needs the squirrel, it visits the cat.Since the lion needs the squirrel (from statement 10), the lion would visit the cat.But wait, the cat is already visiting the lion (statement 3), so that makes sense.\n\nNow, statement 15 says if something chases the squirrel and it is young, then the squirrel needs the cat.The squirrel is young (statement 11), so if something (maybe the cat?)chases it, then the squirrel needs the cat.But I\'m not sure if the cat is chasing the squirrel yet.\n\nStatement 17 says if the cat ignores the squirrel and the cat is blue, then the cat avoids the cow. But I don\'t know if the cat has ignored the squirrel yet. Let\'s not see.\n\nStatement 16 says if something ignores the cow, then it is gentle. The cow is mean (statement 4), so if something ignores the cow, that something is gentle. But I don\'t know who is ignoring the cow yet.\n\nStatement 18 says all mean things are small. So, if something is mean, it\'s small. The cat is mean (statement 1), so the cat is small. The cow is mean (statement 4), so the cow is small. The lion is mean (statement 6), so the lion is small. The squirrel is mentioned as mean yet, but maybe we can\'t figure it out.\n\nStatement 19 says if something is small, then it ignores the squirrel. Since the cat, cow, and lion are all small, they each ignore the squirrel. So, the cat ignores the squirrel, the cow ignores the squirrel, and the lion ignores the squirrel.\n\nNow, statement 20 says if something ignores the cat and the cat ignores the squirrel, then the squirrel is mean. So, if the cat ignores the squirrel, and the cat is ignored by the squirrel (since the cat is small and ignores the squirrel), then the squirrel is mean.\n\nWait, but the cat is ignoring the squirrel because the cat is small (statement 19). So, the cat ignores the squirrel, and the squirrel is ignored by the cat. Therefore, according to statement 20, the squirrel is mean.\n\nNow, since the squirrel is mean (from statement 20), statement 18 says all mean things are small. Therefore, the squirrel is small.\n\nBut the question is asking if the squirrel is big. So, if the squirrel is small, then the statement "The squirrel is big" would be true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The squirrel is old (statement 11), but being old doesn\'t necessarily mean it\'s big. The context doesn\'t say anything about the size of the squirrel beyond being old. However, from the reasoning above, since the squirrel is mean, it must be small (statement 18). Therefore, the squirrel is small, making the statement "The squirrel is big" true.\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']] 55%|#####4    | 329/600 [19:20<24:27,  5.41s/it] 55%|#####5    | 330/600 [19:22<20:00,  4.45s/it]
