messages:  [['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C".\n####\n# Context:\nThe cat is big. The cat is cold. The cat likes the cow. The cat visits the lion. The cow is blue. The cow likes the lion. The cow does not visit the lion. The lion chases the cat. The lion is not big. The lion is rough. The lion visits the cat. The squirrel likes the cow. If something visits the squirrel then it likes the cat. If something visits the cat then it likes the cat. If something likes the squirrel and it is big then the squirrel likes the lion. If something likes the cow and it visits the cow then the cow visits the cat. If something likes the cat then it chases the squirrel. If something chases the lion and the lion is not cold then it visits the cow. If something chases the lion then the lion chases the cow. If something chases the squirrel then the squirrel visits the cat. If the cow is rough then the cow chases the cat.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The squirrel does not chase the squirrel.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The squirrel does not chase the squirrel" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given statements to make it easier to reference:.\n\n1.The cat is big.\n2.The cat is cold.\n3.The cat likes the cow.\n4.The cat visits the lion.\n5.The cow is blue.\n6.The cow likes the lion.\n7.The cow does not visit the lion.\n8.The lion chases the cat.\n9.The lion is not big.\n10.The lion is rough.\n11.The lion visits the cat.\n12.The squirrel likes the cow.\n13.If something visits the squirrel, then it likes the cat.\n14.If something visits the cat, then it likes the cat.\n15.If something likes the squirrel and it is big, then the squirrel likes the lion.\n16.If something likes the cow and it visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat.\n17.If something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel.\n18.If something chases the lion and the lion is not cold, then it visits the cow.\n19.If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow.\n20.If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat.\n21.If the cow is rough, then the cow chases the cat.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the squirrel does not chase the squirrel.So, I need to see if there\'s any information that directly or indirectly tells us about the squirrel\'s actions regarding chasing itself.\n\nLooking through the statements, I don\'t see any direct mention of the squirrel chasing itself.Let\'s check each statement to see if any of them imply that.\n\nStatement 13: If nothing avoids the squirrel, then it dislikes the cat. This tells us about what fails to happen when nothing avoids the squirrel, but not about the squirrel itself.\n\nStatement 14: If nothing avoids the cat, then it dislikes the cat. Again, this is about what fails to happen when nothing avoids the cat, not the squirrel.\n\nStatement 15: If nothing dislikes the squirrel and it is small, then the squirrel dislikes the lion. This is about the relationship between disliking the squirrel and disliking the lion, but only for small things.\n\nStatement 16: If nothing dislikes the cow and it avoids the cow, then the cow avoids the cat. This is about the cow\'s behavior when disliked or avoided.\n\nStatement 17: If nothing dislikes the cat, then it ignores the squirrel. This is about what nothing does if it dislikes the cat.\n\nStatement 18: If nothing ignores the lion and the lion is cold, then it avoids the cow. This is about the lion\'s actions affecting the cow.\n\nStatement 19: If nothing ignores the lion, then the lion ignores the cow. This is about the lion\'s ignoring behavior.\n\nStatement 20: If nothing ignores the squirrel, then the squirrel avoids the cat. This is about the squirrel\'s behavior when nothing ignores it.\n\nStatement 21: If the cow is smooth, then the cow ignores the cat. This is about the cow\'s smoothness leading to ignoring the cat.\n\nNow, all of these statements directly address the squirrel\'s action of ignoring itself. There\'s a statement that talks about the squirrel ignoring the squirrel. The closest is statement 20, which talks about if nothing ignores the squirrel, then the squirrel avoids the cat. But that\'s about nothing else, not the squirrel itself.\n\nSo, since there\'s ample information given about the squirrel ignoring itself, we can determine whether it\'s false or true.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']] 57%|#####6    | 342/600 [20:36<14:17,  3.33s/it] 57%|#####7    | 343/600 [20:38<12:49,  3.00s/it]
