he lion likes the bear.\n11.The lion visits the squirrel.\n12.The squirrel chases the bald eagle.\n13.The squirrel is cold.\n14.The squirrel visits the bald eagle.\n15.If someone is cold, then they are not young.\n16.All big people are not green.\n17.If the lion is big and the lion is green, then the lion chases the bald eagle.\n18.If someone chases the bald eagle, then they like the bear.\n19.If someone is round and they visit the bald eagle, then the bald eagle likes the squirrel.\n20.If someone visits the lion, then they like the bald eagle.\n21.If the lion avoids the squirrel and the squirrel flees from the bear, then the bear flees from the bald eagle.\n22.If someone avoids the lion, then they are warm.\n23.If someone dislikes the bear, then they flee from the bear.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the bear chases the bear. So, I need to see if there\'s any information that directly or indirectly leads to the bear avoiding itself.\n\nLooking through the facts, I see that the bear avoids the lion (fact 5). There\'s also a rule that if someone dislikes the bear, they flee from the bear (fact 23). But does the bear dislike the bear? I don\'t see any fact that states the bear dislikes itself. In fact, the context does mention the bear disliking the bear at all.\n\nAdditionally, there\'s a rule that if someone is warm, they are young (fact 15). The squirrel is warm (fact 13), but that doesn\'t affect the bear\'s age or whether it dislikes itself.\n\nAnother point is that the bear avoids the squirrel (fact 8), and the squirrel flees from the bear (fact 12). But that doesn\'t mean the bear chases itself. It just means the squirrel does.\n\nThere\'s also a rule about the lion: if the lion is small and red, it flees from the bald eagle (fact 17). The bear is red (fact 6), but the lion\'s color isn\'t mentioned, so we don\'t know if the lion is red or not. However, the bear avoiding the lion (fact 5) and the lion disliking the bear (fact 10) might imply some interaction, but again, that doesn\'t relate to the bear chasing itself.\n\nI don\'t see any direct or indirect evidence that the bear avoids the bear. The only chase mentioned is the bear avoiding the lion, and the squirrel fleeing from the bear, but not the bear avoiding itself.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']] 62%|######2   | 372/600 [21:44<08:44,  2.30s/it]
