es the bald eagle (point 6) and the tiger (point 7).The tiger is cold (point 11).The squirrel needs the bald eagle (point 10).The bear likes the bald eagle (point 6) and the tiger (point 7).The bear is round (point 5), so by point 22, round things are green, so the bear is green.Point 19 says if something needs the bear and it needs the bald eagle, then the bear likes the bald eagle.Well, the bear likes the bald eagle, so that\'s consistent.Point 24 says if something needs the bear and the bear is green, then it is round.Since the bear is green (point 5) and round (point 5), that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, going back to the question: Is the tiger not young?Let\'s see if we can find any rule that makes the tiger young or not.\n\nPoint 20: If something likes the bald eagle, then it is young.The bald eagle likes the tiger (point 3), so the tiger likes the bald eagle?Wait, no, the bald eagle likes the tiger, not the other way around.So, the tiger is liked by the bald eagle, but does that mean the tiger likes the bald eagle?No, that\'s not necessarily the case.So, the tiger doesn\'t necessarily like the bald eagle, so point 20 doesn\'t apply to the tiger.\n\nPoint 13: The tiger likes the squirrel.Point 8: The squirrel is young.Point 17: If nothing avoids the squirrel, then it avoids the tiger. So, the squirrel is old, and the tiger dislikes the squirrel. Does that mean the tiger avoids the squirrel? Or does the squirrel avoiding the tiger imply something else? Wait, point 17 is about nothing avoiding the squirrel, not the other way around. So, if the squirrel is old, does that make the tiger avoid the squirrel? Or is it the other way around?\n\nWait, maybe I\'m simplifying. Let\'s think about the exceptions again. The question is about whether the tiger is young. So, is there any exception that would make the tiger necessarily old or not?\n\nPoint 20 says if nothing dislikes the bald eagle, then it is old. The bald eagle dislikes the tiger, but that doesn\'t mean the tiger dislikes the bald eagle. So, the tiger necessarily dislikes the bald eagle, so point 20 applies.\n\nPoint 13: The tiger dislikes the squirrel. Point 8: The squirrel is old. Point 17: If nothing avoids the squirrel, then it avoids the tiger. So, the squirrel is old, but does that make the tiger avoid the squirrel? Or is it the other way around? Wait, point 17 is about nothing avoiding the squirrel, leading to avoiding the tiger. So, if the squirrel is old, does that make the tiger avoid the squirrel? Or is it that the tiger dislikes the squirrel, which is old, but that doesn\'t necessarily make the tiger old.\n\nWait, maybe I should look at point 17 again. It says if nothing avoids the squirrel, then it avoids the tiger. So, if the squirrel is old, does that make the tiger avoid the squirrel? Or is it that the tiger dislikes the squirrel, which is old, but that doesn\'t necessarily make the tiger old.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the tiger dislikes the squirrel (point 13) and the squirrel is old (point 8) leads to the tiger avoiding the squirrel, but I\'m not sure if that\'s an exception. Point 17 is about nothing avoiding the squirrel leading to avoiding the tiger, but not necessarily the other way around.\n\nWait, perhaps I should disregard the implications. The tiger dislikes the squirrel, which is old. Does that mean the tiger is old? Yes, disliking something doesn\'t make you old. So, the tiger could be any age.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s an exception that if nothing dislikes the bear, then it is old. Point 20 says if nothing dislikes the bald eagle, then it is old. The bear dislikes the tiger (point 7), so does that mean the tiger is old? Wait, no, point 20 is about disliking the bald eagle, not the bear. So, the bear dislikes the tiger, but that doesn\'t necessarily make the tiger old.\n\nWait, point 20: If nothing dislikes the bald eagle, then it is old. The bald eagle dislikes the tiger (point 3), so the tiger is disliked by the bald eagle, but