ent 12, all green things are not blue. So if something is green, it cannot be blue. But Charlie is not blue, so does that mean Charlie could be green? Wait, statement 3 says Charlie is green. So Charlie is not blue but green. That\'s impossible because being blue necessarily makes it not green, but all greens are not blue. So Charlie is not blue and green.\n\nNow, looking at statement 16: All white things are not green. So if something is white, it cannot be green. But from statement 3, Charlie is green, so Charlie can be white because all whites are not green. Therefore, Charlie is white.\n\nFrom statement 13: If something is young, then it is not white. So being young implies not being white. But we just concluded that Charlie is white, so Charlie can be young. Because if Charlie were young, it would not have to be white, but it is. Therefore, Charlie is young.\n\nWait, let me check if there\'s any other information that might affect this. Statement 15 says if Erin is mean and not blue, then Erin is not young. But we do know if Erin is not blue or not. Erin is not young (statement 5), but we do know her color. Statement 7 says Harry is not white, and from statement 14, if Harry is not white, then he\'s not young, which he is not. So that\'s inconsistent.\n\nSo putting it all together: Charlie is not blue, green, white, and since being young implies not being white, and Charlie is white, Charlie can be young.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
