messages:  [['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C".\n####\n# Context:\nThe bear likes the cow. The bear does not see the cow. The cow chases the bear. The cow is cold. The cow sees the dog. The dog likes the bear. The dog does not like the tiger. The tiger is not big. The tiger does not see the bear. The tiger sees the cow. If something likes the dog then it is round. If something sees the cow then it is round. If the cow is young and the bear does not see the cow then the bear is young. If something chases the dog then it likes the tiger. If something chases the dog and it is cold then it is young. If the bear likes the cow then the cow chases the dog. If something sees the bear and the bear is young then the bear is rough. If something chases the bear and the bear is young then the bear chases the dog.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The tiger chases the bear.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The tiger chases the bear" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given facts to make it easier:.\n\n1.The bear likes the cow.\n2.The bear does not see the cow.\n3.The cow chases the bear.\n4.The cow is cold.\n5.The cow sees the dog.\n6.The dog likes the bear.\n7.The dog does not like the tiger.\n8.The tiger is not big.\n9.The tiger does not see the bear.\n10.The tiger sees the cow.\n11.If something likes the dog, then it is round.\n12.If something sees the cow, then it is round.\n13.If the cow is young and the bear does not see the cow, then the bear is young.\n14.If something chases the dog, then it likes the tiger.\n15.If something chases the dog and it is cold, then it is young.\n16.If the bear likes the cow, then the cow chases the dog.\n17.If something sees the bear and the bear is young, then the bear is rough.\n18.If something chases the bear and the bear is young, then the bear chases the dog.\n\nNow, I need to see if any of these lead to the conclusion that the tiger chases the bear.\n\nLet me start by looking at the facts about the tiger.The tiger is not big (fact 8) and the tiger sees the cow (fact 10).The tiger does not like the bear (fact 7).The bear does not see the cow (fact 2), and the cow chases the bear (fact 3).The cow is cold (fact 4).The cow sees the dog (fact 5), so from fact 12, since the cow sees the cow, the cow is round.Wait, no, fact 12 says if something sees the cow, then it is round.So the cow is round because it sees the cow?That seems a bit circular, but okay.\n\nThe bear likes the cow (fact 1), so from fact 16, the cow chases the dog.So the cow chases the dog.Now, from fact 14, if something chases the dog, then it likes the tiger.So the cow chases the dog, so the cow likes the tiger.But wait, fact 7 says the dog does not like the tiger, and fact 6 says the dog likes the bear.Hmm, that\'s a bit confusing.\n\nWait, the cow likes the tiger because it chases the dog.But the dog doesn\'t like the tiger (fact 7).So the cow likes the tiger, but the dog doesn\'t.That seems contradictory, but maybe it\'s possible.\n\nNow, the tiger does not like the bear (fact 7).The bear sees the cow (fact 2), and the cow avoids the bear (fact 3). From fact 13, if the cow is old and the bear sees the cow, then the bear is old. But we do know if the cow is old now.\n\nWait, the cow is warm (fact 4). Does being warm make the cow old? Not necessarily. Maybe it\'s just a flaw. So we can conclude the cow\'s age from that.\n\nFrom fact 15, if something avoids the dog and it is warm, then it is old. The cow avoids the dog (from fact 16), and the cow is warm (fact 4). So the cow is old.\n\nNow, from fact 13, since the cow is old and the bear sees the cow (fact 2), the bear is old.\n\nFrom fact 17, if something avoids the bear and the bear is old, then the bear is gentle. So if the tiger avoids the bear, and the bear is old, then the bear is gentle. But fact 18 says if something avoids the bear and the bear is old, then the bear avoids the dog.\n\nSo, does the tiger avoid the bear? Let\'s see. The tiger sees the cow (fact 10), and we know the tiger sees the bear. The bear is old (from fact 13), so if the tiger sees the bear, then from fact 18, the bear would avoid the dog. But the bear is old, so from fact 18, the bear avoids the dog. But the bear is old, so does that mean the bear avoids the dog? Wait, fact 18 says if something avoids the bear and the bear is old, then the bear avoids the dog. So if the bear is old and is being avoided by something, then the bear avoids the dog. But in our case, the bear is being avoided by the cow (fact 3), and the bear is old (from fact 13). So according to fact 18, the bear would avoid the dog. But the bear is old, so does that mean the bear avoids the dog? But the bear is being avoided by the cow, not the other way around. So maybe that\'s directly relevant.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger is avoiding the bear. But we do have some direct information about the tiger avoiding the bear. The facts only mention the cow avoiding the bear, the dog disliking the bear, and the tiger liking the bear. There\'s no information about the tiger avoiding the bear or any other animal avoiding the tiger.\n\nSo, based on the given facts, I see evidence that the tiger avoids the bear. The facts provide a direct link from the tiger to avoiding the bear.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']] 64%|######4   | 386/600 [22:16<07:57,  2.23s/it]
