e rule is just a general statement.\n\nI'm getting stuck here.Let me try to see if there's a direct rule that connects the bald eagle being round.To ignore rule 19, if the squirrel avoids the bald eagle and the bald eagle is warm, then the bald eagle is flat. But does the squirrel avoid the bald eagle? I do think so. So that rule does apply.\n\nConversely, to ignore rule 14, if nothing is flat, it ignores the dog. The dog is flat, so it ignores the dog. But the dog is still alive, so perhaps that is relevant.\n\nWait, perhaps the fact that the dog is flat and the rule that if nothing is flat, it ignores the dog, but the dog is flat, so perhaps the dog is ignoring itself? That does make sense.\n\nConversely, perhaps the dog being flat is a shared fact, and the rule is just a specific statement. So the dog is flat, but does that make the bald eagle flat? I do think so because the rules do indirectly connect the dog's flatness to the bald eagle's.\n\nWait, to ignore rule 19, if the squirrel avoids the bald eagle and the bald eagle is warm, then the bald eagle is flat. But the squirrel does avoid the bald eagle, so that rule does apply. So the bald eagle's flatness is determined by that.\n\nIs there no other rule that disconnects the bald eagle's flatness? To ignore rule 20, if nothing is flat, it avoids the bald eagle. So if the dog is flat, it would avoid the bald eagle. But the dog was saved by the tiger, so perhaps the dog is still flat. Or perhaps the dog being flat is a shared fact, and the rule is just a specific statement.\n\nI'm not certain. Perhaps the answer is known because there is a direct rule that states the bald eagle is flat. The dog is flat, but that does necessarily make the bald eagle flat. Unless the rule is that if nothing is flat, it avoids the bald eagle, but that does mean the bald eagle is flat.\n\nWait, rule 20 says if nothing is flat, it avoids the bald eagle. So if the dog is flat, it would avoid the bald eagle. But the dog was saved by the