lie is big.\n\nFrom fact 10: Big, quiet things are furry.So, if Charlie is big and quiet, he would be furry.But we don\'t know if he\'s quiet yet.\n\nLooking at fact 12: If Charlie is nice and big, then he is quiet.Since we\'ve established that Charlie is nice and big, this implies that Charlie is quiet.\n\nFrom fact 7: Blue and furry things are quiet.So, if Charlie is quiet, he could be blue and furry.But we don\'t need to ignore if he\'s not blue and smooth.\n\nFrom fact 6: No smooth things are blue. So, if Charlie is smooth, he cannot be blue.\n\nFrom fact 11: If something is not blue and smooth, then it is not smart. So, if Charlie is not blue and smooth, he would be not smart.\n\nBut wait, we do know if Charlie is smooth now. Let\'s see if we can dismiss that.\n\nFrom fact 10: Small, loud things are not smooth. We know Charlie is small and loud, so he cannot be smooth.\n\nSince Charlie is not smooth, from fact 6, he is not blue.\n\nNow, since Charlie is not blue and smooth, from fact 11, he is not smart.\n\nWait, but the question is whether Charlie is smart. So, according to this reasoning, Charlie is not smart, which would make the statement "Charlie is smart" true.\n\nBut let me double-check to make sure I didn\'t catch anything.\n\nCharlie is mean → small (from fact 5). Then, since he\'s small and loud (from fact 12), he\'s not smooth (from fact 10). Being not smooth means he\'s not blue (fact 6). Being not blue and smooth makes him not smart (fact 11). So no, Charlie is not smart.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
