, then he is not smart. But Bob is white, so this statement does directly affect him. It\'s more about the case when someone is white.\n\nPoint 10 is the distraction here. It\'s an indirect rule: white people are loud. Since Bob is white, he must be loud. There\'s plenty of information that contradicts this. Let me check if there\'s any other rule that might influence Bob\'s loudness.\n\nPoint 11 says that if someone is mean and loud, then they are not blue. Bob is mean (point 3), and we don\'t know yet if he\'s loud. However, from point 10, we already know he\'s loud because he\'s white. So, according to point 11, Bob should not be blue. But wait, point 6 says Gary is not blue, and point 12 says all white, furry people are not blue. Bob is white, but we don\'t know if he\'s furry. So, Bob could be not blue or not, depending on whether he\'s furry.\n\nBut the question is about Bob being loud, not about his color. So, regardless of whether he\'s not blue or not, since he\'s white, he\'s loud. Therefore, the statement "Bob is loud" should be false.\n\nI do see plenty of contradictions or missing information that would make this statement true.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
