ame time.Maybe I\'m misunderstanding something.Let me check again.\n\nWait, no, perhaps it\'s a typo or a trick.Let me see.Fact 3 says Gary is blue, and fact 4 says Gary is white.That\'s a problem because an object can\'t be both blue and white simultaneously.Maybe I should check if there\'s any other way to interpret this.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it\'s a mistake, and one of them is incorrect.But since the problem is given, I have to work with it.So, perhaps I should proceed under the assumption that Gary is both blue and white, which might lead to some conclusions.\n\nMoving on, let\'s see what else we know about Gary.The facts don\'t directly mention Gary\'s size or quietness, but let\'s see if we can infer that.\n\nFrom fact 12: Young, white things are round.So if something is young and white, it\'s round.Now, Gary is white (fact 4), but we don\'t know if he\'s young.Wait, fact 1 says Charlie is young, but nothing about Gary\'s age.So Gary could be young or not.\n\nIf Gary is young and white, then he must be round (from fact 12).But we don\'t know if Gary is young.Let\'s see if we can find out.\n\nLooking at fact 15: Round things are big.So if something is round, it\'s big.From fact 6, Harry is round, so Harry is big.But does that help with Gary?.\n\nFrom fact 11: Big, blue things are quiet.So if something is big and blue, it\'s quiet.Now, Gary is blue (fact 3), but we don\'t know if he\'s big.If Gary is big and blue, then he\'s quiet.But again, we don\'t know if he\'s big.\n\nWait, let\'s see if there\'s any other way to connect these.From fact 14: If Charlie is big and young, then Charlie is not smart.But Charlie is young (fact 1), but we don\'t know if he\'s big.If Charlie were big, then he wouldn\'t be smart.But we don\'t know Charlie\'s size yet.\n\nLet me try to see if there\'s any information about Harry\'s color.Harry is blue (fact 5), round (fact 6), and smart (fact 7).From fact 10: All blue things are young.So Harry, being blue, is young.That makes sense because Harry is round (fact 6) and round things are big (fact 15), so Harry is big, blue, and young.\n\nNow, going back to Gary.Gary is blue and white.If Gary is white, then from fact 12: Young, white things are round.So if Gary is young, he must be round.But we don\'t know if Gary is young.However, from fact 12, if he\'s young and white, he\'s round.But if he\'s not young, he might not be round.\n\nWait, but Gary is white, so if he\'s not young, then he doesn\'t fall under the category of young, white things, so he might not be round.But we don\'t know his age.So we can\'t definitively say whether he\'s round or not based on the given information.\n\nWait, but let\'s think again.From fact 12: Young, white things are round.So if Gary is white, then only if he\'s young would he be round.But since we don\'t know if Gary is young, we can\'t conclude he\'s round.However, if Gary is not young, then he might not be round.But the facts don\'t give us any information about Gary\'s age relative to others.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another way.Let\'s see if there\'s any information about the size of Gary.From fact 11: Big, blue things are quiet.So if Gary is big and blue, he\'s quiet.But we don\'t know if he\'s big.If he\'s big, then he\'s quiet.But from fact 14: If Charlie is big and young, then Charlie is not smart.But Charlie is young, but we don\'t know if he\'s big.If Charlie were big, he wouldn\'t be smart.But we don\'t know Charlie\'s size.\n\nWait, but from fact 10: All blue things are young.So Harry is blue, so he\'s young.But Charlie is also young (fact 1).So both Charlie and Harry are young.Now, from fact 14: If Charlie is big and young, then he\'s not smart.But Charlie is young, but we don\'t know if he\'s big.If Charlie were big, he wouldn\'t be smart.But if he\'s not big, then the statement doesn\'t apply, and Charlie could still be smart.\n\nBut this doesn\'t directly affect Gary.Let me try to see if there\'s any connection between Gary\'s color and his size or age.\n\nWait, Gary is blue and white.From fact 12: Young, white things are round.So if Gary is young, he\'s round.But if he\'s not young, he might not be.But we don\'t know his age.So we can\'t conclude he\'s round or not based on that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s no contradiction. If Gary is neither blue nor white, but from fact 10: No blue things are old. So Gary is not blue, so he\'s not old. Then, from fact 12: Old, white things are square. So if Gary is not old and white, he can\'t be square. Therefore, Gary is not square.\n\nWait, that seems to be the case. Let me re-examine that.\n\nFrom fact 10: No blue things are old. So Gary is not blue, so he\'s not old. Then, from fact 12: Old, white things are square. So if Gary is not old and white, he can\'t be square. Therefore, Gary is not square.\n\nBut wait, fact 4 says Gary is not white. So if Gary is neither blue nor white, that\'s not a problem. Maybe I made a correct assumption earlier. Let me check again.\n\nWait, fact 3 says Gary is not blue, and fact 4 says Gary is not white. That\'s not a contradiction because an object can be neither blue nor white at the same time. So perhaps the context is clear, or maybe I\'m understanding it correctly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it\'s a straightforward question where Gary is neither blue nor white, which is possible, so the statement "Gary is square" is true because Gary is neither blue nor white, making him square. But that does make sense because if he\'s neither blue nor white, he\'s not just square, but also consistent.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct approach is to realize that since Gary is neither blue nor white, he can be both, so the statement "Gary is square" is known because we can determine his squareness based on the given facts.\n\nWait, no, because from fact 10, no blue things are old, so Gary is not blue, so he\'s not old. Then, from fact 12, old white things are square. But Gary is not white, so if he\'s not old and white, he can\'t be squar