tiger, being young, visits the lion, which it already does (statement 14).\n\nNow, going back to the cat.The cat sees the tiger (statement 4) and the cat visits the lion (statement 6).The cat also visits the bald eagle (statement 5).The cat chases the lion (statement 3).The cat sees the tiger (statement 4).The cat sees the lion (statement 10).Wait, no, statement 10 is the lion sees the cat, not the cat seeing the lion.So, the cat sees the lion?Wait, no, statement 10 says the lion sees the cat, so the cat is being seen by the lion, not the other way around.So, the cat sees the lion?Or does the cat see the lion?Let me check statement 6: The cat visits the lion.So, the cat is visiting the lion, meaning the cat sees the lion.So, the cat sees the lion (statement 6) and the cat sees the tiger (statement 4).The cat also visits the bald eagle (statement 5).\n\nNow, the question is whether the bald eagle chases the cat.From statement 5, the cat visits the bald eagle.From statement 13, the tiger visits the bald eagle.From statement 15, if the tiger sees the lion and the bald eagle, then the tiger chases the cat.Since the tiger does see the lion (statement 10) and the bald eagle (statement 13), the tiger chases the cat (statement 15).So, the tiger chases the cat, which makes the tiger young (statement 16).The tiger, being young, visits the lion (statement 20), which it does (statement 14).\n\nBut does this mean the bald eagle chases the cat?Let\'s see.The cat visits the bald eagle (statement 5).The cat sees the lion (statement 6) and the tiger (statement 4).The cat chases the lion (statement 3).The cat sees the tiger (statement 4).The cat sees the lion (statement 10) via the lion seeing the cat, but that\'s not the cat seeing the lion.Wait, no, statement 10 is the lion seeing the cat, so the cat is being seen by the lion, not the other way around.So, the cat sees the lion?Or does the cat see the lion?Let me clarify.\n\nStatement 6: The cat visits the lion.So, the cat is visiti