g must be cold.Erin is young and cold, which fits.\n4. If Fiona is smooth and warm, then Fiona is foolish. Fiona is smooth and warm, so according to this rule, she must be foolish, which she is not. \n5. Warm, scaly things are black. Both Anne and Erin are warm, scaly, and black, which doesn\'t fit. \n6. All smooth, mean things are old. So if something is smooth and mean, it\'s old. Let\'s see, Erin is smooth, mean, and old. That doesn\'t fit. Fiona is smooth and mean, so according to this rule, Fiona should be old. But wait, the statement we\'re evaluating is "Fiona is young." If the rule says smooth, mean things are old, and Fiona is smooth and mean, then Fiona must be old. So the statement "Fiona is young" would be true. \n\nWait, but let me double-check. The rule says if something is smooth and mean, it\'s old. Fiona is smooth and mean, so she must be old.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
