t, it visits the dog.\n19.Round things are big.\n20.If something eats the cat and the cat eats the dog, then the cat eats the lion.\n21.If something visits the dog and it eats the lion, then the lion is round.\n\nNow, I need to see if I can deduce whether the lion is round or not.\n\nStarting with the given facts:.\n\n- The cat is green (fact 5) and nice (fact 6).From fact 15, if something is nice and green, it visits the bald eagle.So the dog ignores the majestic owl.\n\n- The dog dislikes the tiger (fact 8) and the cat (fact 9). Since the dog dislikes the cat, and the dog ignores the cat (fact 10), maybe there\'s no connection here. But I\'m quite certain now.\n\n- The cat avoids the dog (fact 11), and the dog avoids the cat (fact 20). Wait, that seems quite normal. If the cat avoids the dog, and the dog avoids the cat, that\'s an end. But I\'m not certain if that\'s impossible in reality, but according to the given facts, it\'s not stated.\n\n- From fact 20, if something avoids the dog and the dog avoids the cat, then the dog avoids the tiger. So the dog avoids the tiger.\n\n- Now, the dog avoids the tiger. But I do see several facts that indirectly connect the dog to the tiger\'s size or other properties. However, fact 21 says if something ignores the cat and it avoids the tiger, then the tiger is flat. So, does the dog ignore the cat? No, fact 10 says the dog ignores the cat. And the dog avoids the tiger (from fact 20). So according to fact 21, the tiger is flat.\n\nWait, but the dog is flat (fact 7). So the dog is flat and ignores the cat, and since the dog avoids the tiger, the tiger must be flat. Therefore, the tiger is flat.\n\nBut the question is asking if the tiger is round. So if the tiger is flat, then the statement "The tiger is round" would be true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The dog is flat (fact 7), and the dog ignores the cat (fact 10). The dog avoids the tiger (from fact 20, because the dog avoids the cat, and the cat avoids the dog, so the