n\nWait, point 12 says the squirrel is green, and point 13 says the squirrel is red.That\'s conflicting.So, the squirrel can\'t be both green and red at the same time.That\'s a problem.Maybe I made a mistake in reading the points.\n\nWait, looking back: The squirrel is big (11), green (12), red (13), and sees the bald eagle (14).So, the squirrel is both green and red?That doesn\'t make sense.Maybe it\'s a typo, but I\'ll proceed as given.\n\nAssuming the squirrel is both green and red, which is impossible, but perhaps it\'s a trick.Alternatively, maybe the squirrel is both, but that\'s not possible in reality.Maybe it\'s a mistake, but I\'ll proceed.\n\nPoint 10: The squirrel avoids the bald eagle. So, the squirrel is evading the bald eagle. From point 14, the squirrel ignores the bald eagle. So, the squirrel is neither chasing nor seeing the bald eagle. That\'s impossible.\n\nPoint 15: If someone is blue and they avoid the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is red. The squirrel is blue and avoids the bald eagle, so according to point 15, the bald eagle should be red. But from point 2, the bald eagle is yellow. That\'s not a contradiction.\n\nWait, that\'s not a problem. So, if the squirrel is blue and avoids the bald eagle, then the bald eagle must be red. But the bald eagle is yellow. So, that\'s not a contradiction. Therefore, the assumption that the squirrel is blue must be correct, or perhaps the other points are consistent.\n\nWait, but the question is whether the bald eagle avoids the lion. From the given points, the bald eagle dislikes the lion (point 4), and if someone dislikes the bald eagle, they dislike the lion (point 16). But does disliking the lion mean they avoid it? Not necessarily. So, disliking the lion doesn\'t imply avoiding it.\n\nBut from point 19: If the bald eagle ignores the lion, then the lion ignores the squirrel. So, if the bald eagle ignores the lion, the lion ignores the squirrel. But do we know if the bald eagle ignores the lion?\n\nFrom