thing is mean and not warm, then it is red. But Dave is mean and warm, so this rule does apply to him. Instead, rule 17 tells us that no square things are red. However, Dave is necessarily square; the context mentions that. So, we know if Dave is square or not.\n\nLooking at rule 14, smooth, mean things are square. Since Dave is mean, if he\'s smooth, then he must be square. But we know if Dave is smooth or not. From the given information, Gary is smooth, and there\'s information about Dave\'s smoothness.\n\nRule 7 says Gary is smart, but that tells us everything about Dave\'s foolishness.\n\nSo, putting it all together: Dave is mean and blue, which makes him warm (from rule 16). But we have no information about whether Dave is foolish or not. The only thing we know is that if something is blue and mean, it\'s warm, but everything about foolishness unless it\'s tied to being square or nothing else.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, we can determine if Dave is foolish or not. The statement "Dave is smart" could be false, and we have enough evidence to deny it definitively.\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
