e is blue, so it sees the lion (statement 14).But does the mouse see the squirrel?Or is it the other way around?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse is seeing the squirrel, which would mean the mouse eats the squirrel.But the mouse is blue, so it sees the lion, but does it see the squirrel?The context doesn\'t specify that the mouse is seeing the squirrel, only that the squirrel is visiting the mouse.\n\nWait, the statement says "If something sees the mouse then the mouse eats the squirrel."So, if the squirrel sees the mouse, then the mouse eats the squirrel.But the squirrel is visiting the mouse, which might imply that the squirrel is seeing the mouse, making the mouse eat the squirrel.But the mouse is blue, so it sees the lion, but does it see the squirrel?The context doesn\'t say the mouse is seeing the squirrel, only that the squirrel is visiting the mouse.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse is seeing the squirrel, but that\'s not stated.So, perhaps the mouse isn\'t seeing the squirrel, so the condition in statement 11 isn\'t triggered, meaning the mouse doesn\'t eat the squirrel.Therefore, the squirrel doesn\'t get eaten, so it can visit the mouse without issues.\n\nBut wait, the squirrel is visiting the mouse, and the mouse is blue.So, according to statement 11, if the squirrel sees the mouse, the mouse eats the squirrel.But the squirrel is visiting the mouse, which might mean the squirrel is seeing it, so the mouse would eat the squirrel.But the mouse is blue, so it sees the lion, but does it see the squirrel?The context doesn\'t say the mouse is seeing the squirrel, so maybe the mouse isn\'t seeing it, so the condition isn\'t met, and the mouse doesn\'t eat the squirrel.Therefore, the squirrel can visit the mouse without being eaten.\n\nBut wait, the squirrel is visiting the mouse, and the mouse is blue.So, the mouse is blue, so it sees the lion (statement 14).But the mouse is also being visited by the squirrel, which might mean the mouse is seeing the squirre