e eats the bear."So, bald eagle → eats bear.But then it says, "The bear eats the bald eagle."So, bear → eats bald eagle.So, they have a relationship where each eats the other?That\'s a bit confusing, but okay.\n\nNext, the bald eagle is cold.So, bald eagle → cold.The bald eagle does not like the bear.So, bald eagle → does not like bear.But the bald eagle likes the tiger.So, bald eagle → likes tiger.The tiger is cold.Tiger → cold.The tiger likes the bear.Tiger → likes bear.The mouse likes the tiger.Mouse → likes tiger.The tiger eats the mouse.Tiger → eats mouse.The mouse is kind.If the mouse is kind, then mouse eats bald eagle.So, mouse → if kind, eats bald eagle.\n\nThere\'s a statement about something eating the bald eagle and the bald eagle liking the tiger.If something eats the bald eagle and the bald eagle likes the tiger, then the tiger likes the mouse.So, if (something eats bald eagle) and (bald eagle likes tiger), then tiger likes mouse.But we already know that the tiger likes the bear, so maybe that\'s a separate path.\n\nAnother rule: If something hates the tiger, then it ignores the mouse. So, if something → hates tiger, then something → ignores mouse.\n\nIf the tiger hates the mouse and the mouse does eat the tiger, then the mouse is small. So, tiger → hates mouse, and mouse does eat tiger → mouse is small.\n\nIf something visits the bear and it is cruel, then bear hates tiger. So, if (something visits bear) and (something is cruel), then bear → hates tiger.\n\nHot, cruel things are small. So, if something is hot and cruel, then it\'s small.\n\nIf the tiger avoids the bald eagle, then the tiger does eat the mouse. So, tiger → avoids bald eagle → tiger does eat mouse.\n\nIf something ignores the mouse, then it is cruel. So, if something → ignores mouse, then something is cruel.\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
