hing is cruel and sees the mouse, then it sees the lion.So, if the mouse is cruel, then it sees the lion.But the mouse is already seeing the lion, so that doesn\'t necessarily make the mouse cruel.\n\nAlternatively, if the mouse is cruel, then it sees the lion, which it already does.So, maybe the mouse is cruel.\n\nBut I\'m not sure.Let me think again.\n\nIf the mouse is cruel, then from statement 12, it sees the lion, which it already does.So, being cruel doesn\'t add anything new.\n\nBut if the mouse is cruel, then from statement 15, if something is cruel and sees the mouse, then it sees the lion.But the mouse is already seeing the lion, so that doesn\'t necessarily make the mouse cruel.\n\nWait, maybe the mouse is cruel because it\'s seeing the tiger, who is cruel.But no, statement 9 says if nothing sees the tiger, then the tiger is cruel.It doesn\'t say the something is cruel.\n\nSo, the tiger is cruel because the mouse is seeing it, but that doesn\'t make the mouse cruel.\n\nI\'m getting stuck here.Maybe I should consider if the mouse can see itself, leading to it chasing itself.\n\nBut there\'s no rule that says if something sees itself, then it chases itself.The only rule about chasing is statement 14, which involves the rabbit, lion, and mouse.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse chasing the lion (statement 2) and the lion chasing the mouse (if possible) would lead to the mouse chasing itself.But I don\'t see a rule that connects that.\n\nWait, statement 14 says if something chases the lion and the lion chases the rabbit, then the rabbit chases the mouse.So, if the mouse chases the lion and the lion chases the rabbit, then the rabbit chases the mouse.But that\'s about the rabbit, not the mouse.\n\nSo, unless the mouse is also chasing the rabbit, which I don\'t see happening, I don\'t think we can conclude that the mouse chases itself.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse is chasing the lion, and the lion is chasing the mouse because the mouse is seeing the lion, but that\'s not necessarily true.\n\nI think I\'m overcomplicating this.The question is whether the mouse chases the mouse.There\'s no direct rule that connects the mouse to itself in terms of chasing.The only rule about chasing is statement 2, which is mouse → lion, and statement 14, which is about the rabbit, lion, and mouse.\n\nSo, I don\'t see any logical path that leads to the mouse chasing itself.Therefore, the statement is certain because there\'s no information provided that would allow us to conclude whether the mouse chases itself or not.\n\nWait, but maybe the mouse is cruel, and if it\'s cruel, then from statement 12, if it sees the mouse, it sees the lion.But the mouse is already seeing the lion, so that doesn\'t necessarily make the mouse cruel.\n\nAlternatively, if the mouse is cruel, then it sees the lion, which it already does.So, maybe the mouse is cruel, but that doesn\'t help us with chasing itself.\n\nI think I\'m stuck.I don\'t see any rule that would allow the mouse to not chase itself.\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']] 98%|#########8| 589/600 [35:06<00:27,  2.51s/it]
